.TH TBD 1 "October 7, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tbd \- read a text stub: the exported symbols of a dylib
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tbd
.I stub.tbd
.RB [ \-\-symbol= name ]
.RB [ \-\-symbols ]
.RB [ \-\-reexports ]
.RB [ \-\-objc ]
.RB [ \-\-max= n ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B tbd
.BI \-\-symbol= name
.BI \-\-sdk= dir
.RB [ \-\-mode= exact | substring ]
.RB [ \-\-max= n ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads a
.I .tbd
text stub: the install names, target triples and exported symbols of a dylib.
.PP
A
.I .tbd
is what a Mach\-O symbol table would have been if Apple had shipped it as text.
Since macOS 11 the system dylibs no longer exist as files \fIat all\fR: they are
baked into the dyld shared cache, so
.BR nm (1),
.BR otool (1)
and every tool that reads binaries have nothing to open. The question "what does
libSystem export" is not slow on a modern machine; it is unanswerable by anything
that reads binaries. The answer is in the SDK, as text.
.PP
Reading one needs no Mach\-O parser at all. This command therefore works on a stub
and on nothing else \fIand says so when handed a binary\fR.
.SH ONE FILE, MANY LIBRARIES
A
.I .tbd
is a stream of YAML documents, one per library, and the distinction is load\-bearing.
.B libSystem.tbd
holds
.I 39
of them \fIone per constituent dylib\fR, such as
.IR libsystem_c.dylib ,
.I libsystem_malloc.dylib
and
.IR libsystem_pthread.dylib .
.PP
Reading only the first would report the umbrella's own metadata and a fraction of
its symbols, with nothing in the answer indicating that. So the summary always
states the document count, and
.B \-\-symbol
attributes every hit to the document that exports it. "Which library provides
this symbol" is only answerable if the symbol knows which of the 39 it came from.
.SH SEARCHING AN SDK
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol= name " " \-\-sdk= dir
Report which stubs under
.I dir
export
.IR name .
.PP
This is the question that has no other answer on a current macOS, and it takes
about a second.
.PP
.B 2,448
of the
.I 5,309
stubs in the macOS 15.2 SDK are symlinks \fIone stub per interface umbrella, many
names\fR, so paths are resolved and deduplicated. Without that,
.B _pthread_mutex_lock
is reported as exported by 39 files and a reader concludes there are 39 places to
look; the install name is identical in all 39, so nothing in the output
contradicts it. The alias count is reported rather than hidden.
.SH QUERIES
.TP
.BI \-\-symbol= name
Is this name exported, by which library, and for which targets.
.TP
.B \-\-symbols
List every exported symbol. Use with
.B \-\-max
to cap it; the count stays exact.
.TP
.B \-\-reexports
List the libraries and symbols this stub re\-exports.
.TP
.B \-\-objc
List Objective\-C class and ivar
.IR names .
No types, no ivar offsets, no method lists \fIthis reads no Objective\-C
metadata\fR.
.PP
With none of them, the stub is summarised: version, targets, document count,
symbol count, and one line per library.
.SH A PARTIAL ANSWER IS WITHHELD
A stub containing a line this reader does not understand has an
.I unknown
symbol count. Printing the count anyway states something not known to be true, and
"this stub exports 4,100 symbols" sends someone down the wrong path.
.PP
So the command exits
.B 3
and names the lines, which is also the only way anyone finds out the parser needs
extending. Every line is parsed or the answer is not given.
.SH "WHAT IS NOT READ"
Symbol
.I names
only. No address, because a stub describes the linker's view and contains no
addresses; no Objective\-C types or method lists; no Swift conformance metadata;
no
.I $REF
re\-export target resolution; and not Mach\-O, DWARF or a shared cache. A
re\-export is recorded where the name passes through, and the implementation may
live in another library entirely.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Ran, found something.
.TP
.B 1
Ran, found nothing. This stub does not export that name \fIa negative answer, not
an error\fR, and a caller that retries variations of a name which is genuinely
absent is wasting time.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.TP
.B 3
Could not do the job: the file could not be read, it is not a stub, or it contains
a line this reader does not understand.
.SH EXAMPLES
What does libz export, and where does it install:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-explorer tbd \-\-symbol=_deflate \-\-sdk=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/SDKs/MacOSX15.2.sdk/usr/lib
.fi
.RE
.PP
Which library provides malloc:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-explorer tbd \-\-symbol=_malloc \-\-sdk=$SDK/usr/lib
.fi
.RE
.PP
Summarise one stub, and list its Objective\-C classes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-explorer tbd libz.tbd
macho\-explorer tbd libFoo.tbd \-\-objc
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR otool (1),
.BR nm (1),
.BR dyld_info (1)
.PP
Full documentation: https://ranjithrajv.github.io/MachO-explorer/docs/
